Ectopic pregnancy
Acute Urgent Care
Severe unilateral pelvic pain, Amenorrhoea/Vaginal bleeding, Adnexal mass, Positive beta-hCG

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-028
Reason for Visit: Sudden, severe, sharp pain in the lower abdomen accompanied by lightheadedness and vaginal spotting.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a female of childbearing age presenting with sudden, severe unilateral pelvic pain and presyncope after 6 weeks of amenorrhoea. This presentation carries a high index of suspicion for an ectopic pregnancy. Urgent, immediate face-to-face assessment was required to assess haemodynamic stability and rapidly coordinate emergency transport for definitive surgical evaluation, as waiting for a morning GP visit could result in catastrophic internal haemorrhage.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I had this sudden, ripping pain on my right side down low, and I felt so dizzy I almost passed out.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with the sudden onset of severe, sharp, unilateral right lower abdominal and pelvic pain that began 2 hours ago. The patient reports a preceding period of secondary amenorrhoea lasting approximately 6 weeks, though she assumed her cycle was irregular. She noted abnormal, scant, dark vaginal bleeding starting yesterday. The pain is continuous and excruciating. The patient describes a recent episode of presyncope when attempting to walk to the bathroom, accompanied by a new onset of right shoulder tip pain, highly suggestive of diaphragmatic irritation from accumulating haemoperitoneum.
Functional Status: Lying completely still on the bed, highly anxious, unable to stand without severe dizziness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Imminent risk of life-threatening internal bleeding.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/60 | HR: 115 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Patient appears pale, diaphoretic, and is in severe distress, guarding the lower abdomen.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals marked localized tenderness in the right iliac fossa and suprapubic region, with voluntary guarding. Rebound tenderness is present, suggesting peritoneal irritation. Speculum and bimanual exams (deferred in this acute home setting to prevent precipitating massive rupture, but classic signs noted) would typically reveal profound cervical motion tenderness and a palpable, tender right adnexal mass. Cardiovascular exam demonstrates resting tachycardia and borderline hypotension. Expected investigations include a positive quantitative serum beta-hCG, and a transvaginal ultrasound demonstrating an empty uterine cavity with a complex adnexal mass and free pelvic fluid.
Environmental Observations: Clean towels on the bed; patient's partner is highly distressed.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Took Paracetamol with no relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Ectopic pregnancy - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of amenorrhoea, vaginal bleeding, and sudden onset of pain accompanied by presyncope and shoulder tip pain (signs of internal bleeding rather than simple infection).

Ruptured Ovarian Cyst - Ruled out/unlikely due to the period of amenorrhoea and the classic signs of significant volume loss (tachycardia, presyncope) which are less common with simple cyst ruptures, though a positive pregnancy test is required to definitively distinguish.

Pelvic Inflammatory Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to the acute, sudden onset of the pain, absence of high fever, and the presence of haemodynamic instability.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic tetrad of an ectopic pregnancy: severe unilateral pelvic pain, a period of amenorrhoea with vaginal bleeding, signs of an adnexal mass (inferred from severe right-sided tenderness), and haemodynamic compromise indicating likely early leakage or rupture. The presence of shoulder tip pain confirms diaphragmatic irritation from free blood in the abdomen. An urgent after-hours assessment was unequivocally mandated to confirm the signs of internal bleeding and immediately activate a Priority 1 ambulance transfer. Delaying this assessment would have resulted in catastrophic haemorrhagic shock and death.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed immediate bedside urine pregnancy test (Result: Positive). Administered Morphine 5mg subcutaneously for severe pain. Kept patient strictly Nil By Mouth (NBM). Placed patient supine with legs elevated. Called 000 for Priority 1 ambulance transport to a hospital with obstetrics/gynecology and surgical capabilities.
Positive Behavioral Change: By seeking immediate emergency medical care the moment you experienced severe pelvic pain and dizziness, you have ensured you will receive the life-saving surgical intervention needed to stop the internal bleeding.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Ambulance transport.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to emergency department and gynecology team.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched. The partner is instructed to monitor the patient's breathing and consciousness. If she becomes unresponsive, immediately lay her flat on the floor, raise her legs higher, and update 000.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The positive pregnancy test and severe pain strongly suggest a pregnancy growing outside the womb, which is bleeding internally. This is a surgical emergency.
Clinical Course: The hospital will perform an immediate ultrasound. If an ectopic pregnancy is confirmed, you will likely require emergency surgery to remove it and stop the bleeding.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not try to get up or walk. The bleeding inside your abdomen is dropping your blood pressure, and standing will cause you to faint. We are keeping your legs elevated to protect your brain and heart.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: I have given you a strong painkiller to help you cope with the transfer. We must keep your stomach completely empty because you will likely need general anesthesia for surgery very soon.
Actionable Steps: Try to remain as calm and still as possible. Let your partner pack a small bag with your ID. The paramedics will move you gently onto their stretcher.

